#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Selective `uip` shim for the BYOG admin-configuration smoke task.

Serves the two read paths the task needs (`guardrails byo-configurations
list`, `is connections list`/`ping`) and the write path it grades
(`byo-configurations create`, plus `probe` / `list-validators` / `delete`).
Every other invocation exec's the REAL `uip` found later on PATH.

Why mock: the graded behavior is which commands the skill drives and in
what order, not tenant state. Without a mock the task has to tell the
agent "commands will fail with auth errors, run them anyway" — coaching
that no real user would write and that makes the run a test of prompt
compliance instead of a test of the skill. With the CLI answering, the
prompt is just what an admin would actually ask, and whether the agent
gets there is entirely down to the skill.

`create` really enforces the probe (see probe_pair): a fabricated or
placeholder --connection-id fails with ByoGuardrailProbeFailed and
persists nothing, exactly like the shipped CLI. So the agent cannot pass
by inventing a GUID — it has to discover the connection first.

Created records persist to `.byog-state.json` for the rest of the run, so a
read-back `list` after a create shows what was just created instead of
contradicting it.

Response shapes are copied from the CLI's own documented examples in
packages/admin/guardrails-tool/src/commands/byo-configurations.ts, so the
fields the skill teaches are the fields that come back. Data responses are
always JSON regardless of `--output` (the skill tells the agent to pass it
anyway); `--help` is handed to the real CLI so flag questions get real
answers.

`mock_path_dirs: [mocks]` PATH-prepends this script's directory inside the
sandbox. Linux smoke only (no `windows` tag), hence no `uip.cmd` twin —
same as the ixp and byog_pinning mocks.
"""

import json
import os
import shutil
import sys

MOCK_DIR = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))

